WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.939 --> 00:00:37.570
In today’s video, we’ll be discussing
the military achievements of an outstanding

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:43.309
cast of characters: a Finnish officer who
fought the Red Army on skis; an American Green

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:50.030
Beret who led dangerous covert missions from
Iran to Vietnam; and the only Waffen SS soldier

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:54.350
to be buried at the Arlington military cemetery
in Virginia.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:59.589
What is truly unique, though, is that all
these personas were played by just one, singular

00:00:59.589 --> 00:01:06.340
person: a natural-born soldier who often ended
up on the losing side -- captured, betrayed,

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:08.420
exiled -- but never defeated.

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:14.000
He would rise from the ashes of war to continue
his personal struggle against his sworn enemies.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:19.430
This is the story of Lauri Torni, aka Larry
Thorne: The Phoenix Soldier.

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:20.430
Son of Finland

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.240
[Note: this quote should appear on screen,
against a simple background, but not read

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:23.330
aloud]
“And through the bitter cold with opened

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:24.560
eyes
You’ll find the strength to fight and stand

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:25.660
upright”
- Born in Winter, J and M Duplantier

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:32.900
Lauri Torni was born on May 25, 1919, in Viipuri,
a town on the Karelian isthmus just north

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:33.960
of Petrograd.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:38.750
Lauri completed his studies, excelling mainly
in sports: skiing and boxing, among others.

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:42.970
As he came of age, Lauri joined the national
reserve of the Finnish Army.

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:48.380
Following the partitioning of Poland by Germany
and the Soviets in September of 1939, the

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:53.430
Soviet Union hoped to push its borders farther
west by capturing the Karelian Isthmus and

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:57.680
securing Leningrad, formerly Petrograd, from
a German attack.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:02.880
The Soviets had also set their sights on several
islands in the Gulf of Finland, and on the

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:04.350
Hanko naval base.

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:08.590
When Finland refused to grant such an annexation,
Joseph Stalin ordered the invasion.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:15.030
Thus, a few months after Lauri’s 20th Birthday,
on November 30, 1939, the Soviet Union invaded

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:16.030
Finland.

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:19.140
It was the beginning of the Russo-Finnish
War, also known as The Winter War.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:24.060
The Finnish armed forces were led by Marshall
Carl Gustav Emil Mannerheim, a former czarist

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:25.060
officer.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:28.170
Severely outnumbered and outgunned, the Finns
managed to put up one hell of a fight against

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:32.810
the Red Army – a huge opponent, but one
that was ill-equipped and poorly managed after

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:37.190
Stalin had executed dozens of his top Generals
during the Great Purge of 1937.

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:41.510
The Finnish Army, by contrast, was a small,
flexible unit that had perfected the art of

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:45.790
winter warfare and defensive tactics, exacting
a huge toll on invaders.

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:51.690
At the beginning of the war, young Lauri was
a junior sergeant in charge of food supplies.

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:55.170
Lauri couldn’t have felt at ease with this
post -- he clearly understood how a constant

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:59.530
supply of nourishment was vital for the morale
of his fellow troops, but he thought he was

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:02.770
capable of much more than supply line management.

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:07.901
One night, as the temperature plummeted to
minus-42 Celsius, Lauri silently slid out

00:03:07.901 --> 00:03:09.260
of his camp.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:14.550
Completely unseen and unheard by his own sentinels,
Sergeant Torni walked to a Soviet outpost,

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:17.510
captured a Russian soldier, and marched him
back to the Finnish camp.

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:20.690
The effects on his soldiers’ morale were
galvanising.

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:25.490
Lauri’s superiors realised that this 20-year
old NCO had a talent for light-infantry missions

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:28.450
and assigned him his first special task.

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:33.780
Lauri was put in command of an anti-tank patrol:
basically, it was people fighting on foot,

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:37.910
or on skis, whose mission was to attack Soviet
tanks and destroy them.

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:40.830
Yeah, that sounds easy!

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:45.030
But against any logical bet, Lauri and his
squad did manage to blow up quite a few tanks,

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:46.590
and still lived to tell the story.

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:51.450
Lauri and his Fins distinguished themselves
particularly at the Siege of East Lemetti

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:53.520
pocket, in late February, 1940.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.150
Here, the Finns encircled and wiped out a
strong Red Army contingent, capturing 71 tanks,

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:01.210
206 trucks, and 12 armoured cars.

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:05.420
Lauri was put on a special officer’s course,
becoming a second lieutenant in March.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:09.740
As a first assignment, he was put in charge
of a company of ethnic Swede soldiers.

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:12.240
They did not speak Finnish; he did not speak
Swedish.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:16.739
But with the use of clear hand gestures and
the common language of ‘let’s blow up

00:04:16.739 --> 00:04:20.810
the invaders,’ the young officer and his
troops got along splendidly.

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:24.320
On a broader stage, though, the war was not
going well for Finland.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:29.080
Despite having bloodied the nose of the Soviet
Bear, the disadvantage in terms of manpower,

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:32.440
artillery, and tanks was starting to take
its inevitable toll.

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:36.160
Already by February, the Soviets had breached
the Finns’ main defensive line on the Southern

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:38.400
end of the front, the Mannerheim line.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:43.470
Once they broke through, they were able to
occupy Karelia, and threaten Lauri’s hometown,

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:44.470
Viipuri.

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:48.960
From the 3rd through the 11th of March 1940,
heavy fighting took place around the Karelian

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:53.530
capital, while a Finnish delegation in Moscow
sought to establish terms for a ceasefire.

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:57.910
The Moscow Peace Treaty was eventually signed
at 2 a.m. on March 13.

00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:03.490
Finland had to cede 10% of its territory and
12% of its population to the Soviet Union,

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:05.810
including Karelia and Viipuri.

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:12.150
It had been a pyrrhic victory for the Red
Army, which suffered 167,000 KIA and MIA,

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:14.840
vs 26,000 on the Finnish side.

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:20.590
But it had been a victory nonetheless, one
that Lauri Torni would never forget, and never

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:21.830
forgive.

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:29.780
Soviet Enemy Number One
At the end of the conflict, Lauri was awarded

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:34.990
the Medals of Independence, 1st and 2nd Class,
which became the first in a long series of

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:35.990
decorations.

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:39.670
But such accolades could not compensate for
the humiliation of losing one tenth of his

00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:42.810
country to the invaders - including his hometown!

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:48.040
Lauri was described both by friends and army
superiors as quiet, pleasant, and courteous.

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:51.990
He was a well-liked young man with whom it
was easy to get along.

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:57.449
But this mostly mild-mannered man had found
a powerful vocation in fighting against a

00:05:57.449 --> 00:06:01.860
stronger opponent -- either in direct, or
indirect, service to Finland.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:07.320
That is why, in the late Spring of 1941, when
Finland was a neutral country deep in the

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:13.889
background of WWII, Lauri Torni decided to
rise from his country’s defeat and head

00:06:13.889 --> 00:06:15.080
back to battle.

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:20.210
This need for retribution drove him to travel
to Germany in secret and join a newly constituted

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:24.210
Waffen-SS Battalion, the Freiwilligen Nordost.

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:29.040
It was not uncommon for citizens of neutral,
occupied, or even Allied countries to volunteer

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:34.380
for SS formations, either out of sympathy
for the Nazi cause, hatred of Bolshevism,

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:35.610
or both.

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:40.460
As part of the Freiwilligen Nordost, Torni
received intensive training which honed his

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:45.290
natural skill set -- performing special operations
deep inside enemy territory.

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:49.850
On June 22, 1941, Axis forces invaded the
Soviet Union.

00:06:49.850 --> 00:06:52.790
This was the start of Operation Barbarossa.

00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:57.660
Finland joined the invasion, not as a formal
member of the Axis, but as a co-belligerent

00:06:57.660 --> 00:06:58.660
country.

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:03.509
The Finnish objective was simply to recapture
the territories lost in the Treaty of Moscow,

00:07:03.509 --> 00:07:07.570
rather than expand their living space eastward
into new land.

00:07:07.570 --> 00:07:13.479
This conflict became known as The Continuation
War, and was again masterminded by Field Marshall

00:07:13.479 --> 00:07:14.479
Mannerheim.

00:07:14.479 --> 00:07:18.729
The commander asked for Berlin to return the
many Finnish volunteers who had joined the

00:07:18.729 --> 00:07:23.370
Waffen-SS to replenish the Army ranks, with
Lauri Torni chief among them.

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:28.080
At the start of 1942, second Lieutenant Torni
was again put in charge of a light infantry

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:31.949
patrol deployed in Karelia – it was payback
time!

00:07:31.949 --> 00:07:35.910
His unit was part of the forces that drove
the Soviets away from the isthmus and captured

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:38.730
the main garrison of Petrozavodsk.

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:42.880
From there, Torni continued to push northward
to reach the White Sea-Baltic Canal, then

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:43.880
called ‘Stalin Canal’.

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:44.880
[Note: see this map for the locations mentioned
above]

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:48.349
Lauri’s advance was halted on March 23,
1942.

00:07:48.349 --> 00:07:52.750
While on a ski reconnaissance mission, Lauri
stepped over a landmine and was severely wounded.

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:57.169
While recovering in the hospital, Torni received
a promotion to lieutenant and an order to

00:07:57.169 --> 00:08:01.070
stay put for several weeks… which he totally
ignored.

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:04.919
With fragments of shrapnel still embedded
into his body, Torni escaped from the military

00:08:04.919 --> 00:08:07.530
hospital to re-join his old unit.

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:12.940
Throughout 1943, Lauri led and trained several
units in charge of reconnaissance and sabotage

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:15.150
missions deep behind enemy lines.

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:19.350
An exceptionally fit man, he expected his
recruits to match his levels of strength,

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:24.639
stamina, resilience, and skill in close quarters
combat, especially with the puukko -- the

00:08:24.639 --> 00:08:25.930
traditional Finnish knife.

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:30.500
His company became known as the Osasto Torni,
or the Torni detachment.

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:34.519
Its symbol was a capital ‘T’ set against
the backdrop of yellow lighting.

00:08:34.519 --> 00:08:38.940
Some of Torni’s best-documented exploits
took place in the summer of 1944, when the

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:42.360
Red Army launched a counterattack to retake
the Karelian isthmus.

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:47.490
On June 9 -- three days after D-Day, a couple
thousand miles away -- his Division was at

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:50.530
risk of once again being engulfed by the Soviets.

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:54.850
Lieutenant Torni and his unit managed to break
a Soviet encirclement manoeuvre, inflicting

00:08:54.850 --> 00:08:56.600
heavy casualties on the enemy.

00:08:56.600 --> 00:09:00.910
Two weeks later, Lauri personally performed
an incursion behind enemy lines, during which

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:05.260
he acquired vital intelligence on the Soviet
strategy – intelligence which helped his

00:09:05.260 --> 00:09:08.140
commanders hold off the counterattack in Karelia.

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:12.620
On the 5th and 6th of July, the Torni detachment
was instructed to hold the crucial Ilomansti

00:09:12.620 --> 00:09:14.339
crossroads at all costs.

00:09:14.339 --> 00:09:18.140
They were reduced to less than 100 men after
the beginning of the Soviet offensive, and

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:24.060
they were about to face two regiments of the
Red Army, a total of 4000 enemy soldiers.

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:28.020
Against all military logic, Lauri split his
men into even smaller units.

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:31.560
Sure, they may have been weaker, but they
were also highly mobile.

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:35.560
By shuttling back and forth between them,
Lauri was able to direct a series of counter

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:40.470
attacks against the incoming Soviets, which
were convinced they were facing a much larger

00:09:40.470 --> 00:09:41.470
force.

00:09:41.470 --> 00:09:45.110
Torni’s company inflicted hundreds of losses
on the Red Army, halting their advance and

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:48.310
allowing for the bulk of the Finnish forces
to retreat and regroup.

00:09:48.310 --> 00:09:52.260
It was a tactical masterpiece that achieved
an important strategic result.

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:57.110
But Lauri did not have time to celebrate,
as, once again, he stepped onto a Soviet mine.

00:09:57.110 --> 00:10:01.440
While convalescing, Lauri learned that he
had been made a Knight of the Cross of Mannerheim,

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:05.190
the highest military decoration in the Finnish
armed forces.

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:11.760
It is estimated that a total of 600,000 Finns
served in the Continuation War, but only 191

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:14.140
of them received such high military honour.

00:10:14.140 --> 00:10:16.500
The following month, he was promoted to Captain.

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:20.590
At the same time, the Soviets had bestowed
on Lauri an even higher accolade.

00:10:20.590 --> 00:10:24.899
Propaganda radio broadcasts had issued a bounty
on his head, dead or alive.

00:10:24.899 --> 00:10:29.220
The Soviets were willing to pay three million
Finnish Marks, which corresponds to more than

00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:32.350
half a million US dollars in today’s money.

00:10:32.350 --> 00:10:37.200
It’s a small fortune, especially during
wartime … yet no one dared to collect the

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:38.200
reward.

00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:42.060
Lauri and his men continued the fight in Karelia,
but their Commander-in-Chief, Marshal and

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:45.630
now-President Mannerheim, was coming to terms
with a harsh reality.

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:49.980
The fight could not go on forever against
the Red steamroller, especially now that Germany

00:10:49.980 --> 00:10:52.210
and its allies were losing ground.

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:57.620
On August 25, 1944, the Finnish government
initiated secret talks with Moscow, which

00:10:57.620 --> 00:11:00.850
led to the signing of an armistice on September
19th.

00:11:00.850 --> 00:11:05.460
The conditions, while ensuring the survival
of the nation, were extremely harsh: Finland

00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:09.680
had to cede back 10 percent of its territory,
including Karelia and Viipuri.

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:14.029
Helsinki also had to pay war reparations to
Moscow, equivalent to almost four and a half

00:11:14.029 --> 00:11:15.990
billion US dollars in today’s money.

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:20.550
As if that wasn’t enough, Mannerheim was
forced to demobilize most of his army … and

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:24.330
to expel any remaining Wehrmacht units from
Finnish territory.

00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:29.670
This became known as the Lapland War … one
in which Lauri did not take part, as he had

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:30.670
been demobilized.

00:11:30.670 --> 00:11:36.490
Once again, Lauri Torni wasn’t prepared
to yield amidst the wreckage of another unfair

00:11:36.490 --> 00:11:37.490
defeat.

00:11:37.490 --> 00:11:41.620
Like the mythical phoenix rising from its
own ashes, he was eager to continue the fight

00:11:41.620 --> 00:11:46.260
against the Soviets, whose peace treaty he
did not trust.

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:51.820
Alongside other demobbed troops, Lauri fled
to Hamburg on a U-Boat to resume special operations,

00:11:51.820 --> 00:11:56.420
training in a Waffen-SS facility in Mecklenburg,
along the Baltic Coast.

00:11:56.420 --> 00:12:01.700
Here, Lauri perfected his approach to asymmetric
warfare, including sabotage techniques, explosives

00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:04.330
handling, and deep reconnaissance tactics.

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:08.060
Unfortunately for Torni, the programme was
halted after a few weeks of training because

00:12:08.060 --> 00:12:10.450
the Red Army was making its way towards Berlin.

00:12:10.450 --> 00:12:15.839
The Wehrmacht was in desperate need of combat-ready
troops, and so Captain Torni was drafted:

00:12:15.839 --> 00:12:20.150
he was tasked with leading a Naval Infantry
company, deployed east of Berlin.

00:12:20.150 --> 00:12:24.870
Facing once more the invader of his Homeland,
Torni fought valiantly against the incoming

00:12:24.870 --> 00:12:25.870
enemies.

00:12:25.870 --> 00:12:30.750
But once again, his tactical victories could
not overcome the Soviet’s overwhelming numerical

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:31.890
superiority.

00:12:31.890 --> 00:12:36.790
Facing inevitable capture by the Soviets – which
would have meant almost certain death – Torni

00:12:36.790 --> 00:12:41.910
instead sought to turn himself in as prisoner
to a British unit which had landed in a nearby

00:12:41.910 --> 00:12:42.910
airfield.

00:12:42.910 --> 00:12:45.650
His bet paid off, and the Brits did not kill
him on sight.

00:12:45.650 --> 00:12:50.640
He was sent instead to a PoW camp in Belgium,
then transferred to Lubeck, Germany.

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:56.660
Life as a British prisoner was surely better
than living as an inmate at a Gulag, but rumours

00:12:56.660 --> 00:13:00.710
started spreading that the Western Allies
were transferring some of their PoWs to the

00:13:00.710 --> 00:13:01.710
Soviets.

00:13:01.710 --> 00:13:02.899
Torni was not going to risk that.

00:13:02.899 --> 00:13:08.140
He partnered with a fellow Finn, Solmu Korpela,
and escaped from the camp in July of 1945.

00:13:08.140 --> 00:13:12.310
The circumstances of how they did it are not
clear … what is documented is that they

00:13:12.310 --> 00:13:17.380
posed as former prisoners of the Wehrmacht
to hitch a ride from a British Army Captain!

00:13:17.380 --> 00:13:22.050
Eventually they reached Copenhagen and secured
travel visas from the Finnish consulate.

00:13:22.050 --> 00:13:26.540
Lauri Torni had emerged from the ruins of
the Reich and was finally heading home.

00:13:26.540 --> 00:14:28.139
But what he received was not 
a hero’s welcome.

00:14:28.139 --> 00:14:44.390
A Wanted Man

00:14:44.390 --> 00:15:03.670
When Lauri returned to Finland in August of
1945, he was almost immediately prosecuted

00:15:03.670 --> 00:15:06.670
for treason by the Valpo, the Finnish State
Police.

00:15:06.670 --> 00:15:08.800
Technically, they were not wrong.

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:13.060
Torni had escaped to Hamburg and joined the
Wehrmacht during the Lapland War … in other

00:15:13.060 --> 00:15:15.790
words, he had defected to the enemy!

00:15:15.790 --> 00:15:20.110
The situation was complicated by Finland’s
delicate position between the end of WWII

00:15:20.110 --> 00:15:21.890
and the start of the Cold War.

00:15:21.890 --> 00:15:25.720
While the Red Army could have invaded yet
again – as it had done with almost all Eastern

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:29.589
Europe – Stalin actually preferred a less
aggressive approach.

00:15:29.589 --> 00:15:34.740
He demanded instead that Finland remain a
neutral country, but under a strong Soviet

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:35.740
influence.

00:15:35.740 --> 00:15:39.050
It was a shrewd strategic move, as it would
prevent NATO from deploying future troops

00:15:39.050 --> 00:15:43.700
along the Soviet-Finnish frontier, one of
the longest sections of the Soviet Union’s

00:15:43.700 --> 00:15:44.700
borders.

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:48.839
Moscow’s influence extended to the appointment
of ministers and officials aligned to the

00:15:48.839 --> 00:15:52.199
Communist Party – and one of them was the
chief of the Valpo.

00:15:52.199 --> 00:15:56.699
Anyway, in August of 1945, Torni had been
arrested for treason, and was placed on a

00:15:56.699 --> 00:15:59.220
train headed to a high security prison.

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:04.420
Lauri did not wait for the term to begin;
he simply jumped off the train during a stop.

00:16:04.420 --> 00:16:08.420
For the next few months, Lauri reunited with
his beloved parents and his sister Kaija,

00:16:08.420 --> 00:16:11.880
who had been forcibly relocated from Viipuri
to Helsinki.

00:16:11.880 --> 00:16:16.620
Here, he worked at an electrical supply firm
and generally lived in the open … he enjoyed

00:16:16.620 --> 00:16:20.089
the simple pleasures of life as a free, innocent
man!

00:16:20.089 --> 00:16:24.040
However, after a co-worker reported him to
the Valpo, the former officer was apprehended

00:16:24.040 --> 00:16:25.040
once more.

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:29.709
A trial in October of 1946 sentenced him to
six years in prison for high treason.

00:16:29.709 --> 00:16:35.300
Once again, the only way out of that sticky
situation was to … well, to just leave.

00:16:35.300 --> 00:16:40.370
Torni certainly was no stranger to evasions,
so in the spring of 1947, he simply walked

00:16:40.370 --> 00:16:43.910
out of jail, thanks to a false ID card smuggled
by a friend.

00:16:43.910 --> 00:16:48.370
His life as a fugitive did not last long,
though, as Valpo had an ace up their sleeve.

00:16:48.370 --> 00:16:52.660
The police spread the false rumour that he
was wanted for theft -- a blemish on his upright

00:16:52.660 --> 00:16:55.290
reputation that Torni could not tolerate.

00:16:55.290 --> 00:16:59.600
Willing to clear his record, Lauri handed
himself over voluntarily and returned behind

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:01.690
bars at Turku State Prison.

00:17:01.690 --> 00:17:06.059
But it wasn't long before the restless Captain
was ready to attempt another escape.

00:17:06.059 --> 00:17:10.300
While generally a quiet man, Lauri could make
a quite genial and cultured companion.

00:17:10.300 --> 00:17:13.919
He made friends with the organist in service
at the prison Church.

00:17:13.919 --> 00:17:18.239
While serving as his assistant, he identified
a poorly secured window in the chapel, from

00:17:18.239 --> 00:17:22.959
which he believed he could escape with a classic
tactic: a rope made of bed sheets, tied to

00:17:22.959 --> 00:17:23.959
an iron hook.

00:17:23.959 --> 00:17:28.329
But on the night of the escape, a guard discovered
him and took aim with his rifle, ready to

00:17:28.329 --> 00:17:30.370
end this indomitable soldier’s career.

00:17:30.370 --> 00:17:34.640
Luckily, the gun malfunctioned, and Torni
was apprehended without serious incident.

00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:38.399
By the end of the decade, the Soviet’s grip
on the Finnish Government had loosened enough

00:17:38.399 --> 00:17:41.529
for President Paasikivi to issue a pardon
for Torni.

00:17:41.529 --> 00:17:44.859
Unfortunately, the pardon involved his being
stripped of his rank.

00:17:44.859 --> 00:17:47.909
His name was removed from official war records,
too.

00:17:47.909 --> 00:17:50.090
This was October 6, 1950.

00:17:50.090 --> 00:17:51.779
Lauri felt betrayed.

00:17:51.779 --> 00:17:56.739
He wasn’t sure he had anything else to offer
to his own country, so in 1949, he decided

00:17:56.739 --> 00:17:59.100
to emigrate, crossing the border into Sweden.

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:01.350
Here, he started a new life … as a lumberjack!

00:18:01.350 --> 00:18:02.350
[Note to Simon: the following two lines are
a quote from the famous ‘Lumberjack Song’

00:18:02.350 --> 00:18:03.350
by Monty Python, also linked below.

00:18:03.350 --> 00:18:04.350
Feel free to remove!]

00:18:04.350 --> 00:18:05.350
Yes, he'd be a lumberjack, and be okay.

00:18:05.350 --> 00:18:06.350
He’d sleep all night and work all day,

00:18:06.350 --> 00:18:07.350
He started dating a girl from Stockholm, Marja
Kops, the first and only real love of his

00:18:07.350 --> 00:18:08.350
life.

00:18:08.350 --> 00:18:12.210
The two got engaged on January 1, 1950 and
had even set a date for their wedding.

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:16.489
The two had agreed that Lauri would wear his
old Captain’s uniform for the wedding photo

00:18:16.489 --> 00:18:19.190
shoot, so the girl travelled to Finland to
collect it.

00:18:19.190 --> 00:18:21.149
But fate threw a spanner in the works.

00:18:21.149 --> 00:18:25.059
Lauri had previously applied for a visa to
travel to Venezuela under the alias of Ema

00:18:25.059 --> 00:18:26.059
Mbrsky.

00:18:26.059 --> 00:18:31.190
His intention was to join a community of Finnish
expats in the Americas and start a new life.

00:18:31.190 --> 00:18:33.889
After months of waiting, the visa had finally
arrived.

00:18:33.889 --> 00:18:38.919
Unfortunately, this offered only a very limited
time window for Lauri to leave Sweden.

00:18:38.919 --> 00:18:40.259
Torni … was torn.

00:18:40.259 --> 00:18:42.940
Okay, that was lame, but Lauri was, indeed,
torn between his new love and the opportunity

00:18:42.940 --> 00:18:44.989
to start a new life away from Europe.

00:18:44.989 --> 00:18:47.860
In the end, he opted to leave, before Marja
could return.

00:18:47.860 --> 00:18:52.320
Lauri got in touch and promised her he would
be back, but their connection waned over the

00:18:52.320 --> 00:18:53.320
years.

00:18:53.320 --> 00:18:56.279
Eventually, Marja moved to Las Palmas to marry
a Spanish man.

00:18:56.279 --> 00:19:01.010
Down in South America, Lauri landed in Caracas
in March of 1950, taking on odd jobs around

00:19:01.010 --> 00:19:03.500
the local Finnish community to make ends meet.

00:19:03.500 --> 00:19:07.169
Among these jobs, Lauri would work as a babysitter
for his compatriots.

00:19:07.169 --> 00:19:10.090
And that’s comedy gold, right there, Hollywood!

00:19:10.090 --> 00:19:14.749
A former Waffen-SS Captain and Special Forces
operator looking after young children?

00:19:14.749 --> 00:19:17.059
Send that screenplay to all the major executives.

00:19:17.059 --> 00:19:21.129
Beyond babysitting, a job more suited to Lauri
was as a sailor on merchant ships, sailing

00:19:21.129 --> 00:19:22.129
to the US.

00:19:22.129 --> 00:19:26.059
The Finn had since long set his sights on
moving to the States, but the status of his

00:19:26.059 --> 00:19:29.259
visa did not allow him to step off the boat
onto dry land.

00:19:29.259 --> 00:19:33.360
But a man like Torni would not let a little
bit of paperwork get in his way.

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:37.799
By now an escape artist on par with the Great
Houdini, Lauri simply jumped overboard off

00:19:37.799 --> 00:19:41.390
the coast of Louisiana and swam safely to
New Orleans.

00:19:41.390 --> 00:19:45.690
From there, Lauri made his way to New York,
where he settled in Harlem, resuming his routine

00:19:45.690 --> 00:19:46.690
of odd jobs.

00:19:46.690 --> 00:19:51.220
By 1951, Lauri had been apprehended by FBI
agents: he was an illegal immigrant, after

00:19:51.220 --> 00:19:54.730
all, and it was only a matter of time before
he would be kicked out of the country.

00:19:54.730 --> 00:19:58.649
Luckily, in New York, Torni had become friends
with one Paavo Fleming.

00:19:58.649 --> 00:20:04.149
This gentleman was the President of an association
of Finnish Veterans and Soldiers in the States,

00:20:04.149 --> 00:20:08.479
and he had immediately recognised the great
potential that Lauri could offer to the US

00:20:08.479 --> 00:20:09.679
Armed Forces.

00:20:09.679 --> 00:20:13.649
Fleming had brought Lauri’s case to the
attention of retired General William ‘Wild

00:20:13.649 --> 00:20:18.979
Bill’ Donovan, a key figure within the OSS
and, later, the CIA.

00:20:18.979 --> 00:20:23.330
Now an attorney, Donovan agreed to represent
Torni and was able to secure him a permit

00:20:23.330 --> 00:20:24.330
to remain in the US.

00:20:24.330 --> 00:20:29.070
Not only that, but he interceded for him to
be enrolled in the US Army.

00:20:29.070 --> 00:20:35.470
This is when Lauri Torni reemerged as Larry
Allan Thorne, a legend of the Green Berets.

00:20:35.470 --> 00:20:41.340
All the Wars He Came Across

00:20:41.340 --> 00:20:47.330
In January of 1954, former Captain Torni started
basic training at Fort Dix, New Jersey, as

00:20:47.330 --> 00:20:48.330
Private Thorne.

00:20:48.330 --> 00:20:53.139
He must have felt like a real Finn out of
water: he was 34, while the average age of

00:20:53.139 --> 00:20:57.539
the soldiers there was 19; he had the inclination
to lead, but he also had to re-train as a

00:20:57.539 --> 00:21:02.720
low-ranking soldier; his English was not really
up to scratch, but he did manage to communicate

00:21:02.720 --> 00:21:06.780
at least in part with a blend of hand gestures
and shouted profanities.

00:21:06.780 --> 00:21:10.730
After completing basic training, he attended
two specialisation courses.

00:21:10.730 --> 00:21:14.940
First, there was the Mountain and Arid Cold
Weather Survival Course, which must have felt

00:21:14.940 --> 00:21:16.740
like any other Tuesday to him.

00:21:16.740 --> 00:21:20.960
There was also the paratrooper course at Fort
Benning, Georgia, which earned him a HALO

00:21:20.960 --> 00:21:21.960
certificate.

00:21:21.960 --> 00:21:22.960
[Caption: HALO = High Altitude Low Opening]

00:21:22.960 --> 00:21:26.679
In 1957, Larry was serving in Bavaria, as
a member of the 11th Airborne Division.

00:21:26.679 --> 00:21:31.179
He had performed well in the US Military,
climbing to the rank of 1st Lieutenant.

00:21:31.179 --> 00:21:35.580
However, following several months of relative
quiet, he got into serious trouble.

00:21:35.580 --> 00:21:40.049
In December of 1958, Thorne was involved in
a pub brawl with some locals, a fistfight

00:21:40.049 --> 00:21:44.960
which ended in his throwing several Bavarian
patrons through a plate glass window.

00:21:44.960 --> 00:21:47.249
Larry was arrested and court-martialled.

00:21:47.249 --> 00:21:52.370
He seriously risked his own dismissal from
the Army, but luck was on his side -- investigators

00:21:52.370 --> 00:21:56.070
found that some of the Germans involved in
the brawl had a criminal record, and at least

00:21:56.070 --> 00:21:57.859
two of them were wanted by the police.

00:21:57.859 --> 00:22:02.679
At Thorne's court-martial in October 1959,
he was found not guilty.

00:22:02.679 --> 00:22:06.000
Thorne could return to his duties, but he
preferred to accept a transfer instead, to

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:11.460
the 10th Group of the Special Forces - the
Green Berets - based in Bad Tölz, Germany.

00:22:11.460 --> 00:22:16.960
It was with this team that in July 1962, Lauri
took part in one of his best-known missions.

00:22:16.960 --> 00:22:20.970
His unit was tasked with retrieving the wreckage
of an Otter reconnaissance plane.

00:22:20.970 --> 00:22:25.200
The aircraft had crashed over the Zagros mountains
in Iran, after a secret mission to gather

00:22:25.200 --> 00:22:28.629
intel on Larry Thorne’s old nemesis, the
Soviet Union.

00:22:28.629 --> 00:22:32.679
Two previous crews of German mountaineer troops
had failed to locate the Otter, which contained

00:22:32.679 --> 00:22:36.119
information too sensitive to be left unattended
on a mountain top.

00:22:36.119 --> 00:22:39.759
Under the leadership of Captain Schandler,
Larry and a dozen of Green Berets climbed

00:22:39.759 --> 00:22:44.070
the unforgiving terrain of the Zagros range
and succeeded in reaching the wreckage.

00:22:44.070 --> 00:22:47.609
They retrieved all the classified intel, as
well as the bodies of the airmen who had died

00:22:47.609 --> 00:22:48.610
in the crash.

00:22:48.610 --> 00:22:52.929
Along the way, Larry and friends even found
time to provide medical aid to isolated communities

00:22:52.929 --> 00:22:53.929
on the mountains.

00:22:53.929 --> 00:22:58.590
In early 1963, Lieutenant Thorne was assigned
a mission in South Vietnam.

00:22:58.590 --> 00:23:02.720
As a military advisor, his job was to work
with the South Vietnamese special forces,

00:23:02.720 --> 00:23:05.320
which he led in several airborne missions.

00:23:05.320 --> 00:23:10.320
On one such assignment, Thorne, his Sergeant,
and 10 South Vietnamese militiamen were ambushed

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:12.409
by 120 Viet Congs.

00:23:12.409 --> 00:23:16.649
Larry’s ‘Dirty Dozen’ soundly defeated
the guerrillas, and he was awarded with a

00:23:16.649 --> 00:23:17.779
Bronze Star.

00:23:17.779 --> 00:23:22.609
After this first tour in Vietnam, the now-Captain
Thorne returned to Fort Benning for more advanced

00:23:22.609 --> 00:23:26.899
training, after which he was ordered to a
second tour in Vietnam, during the spring

00:23:26.899 --> 00:23:29.059
and summer 1965.

00:23:29.059 --> 00:23:33.929
Thorne was working at the 5th Special Forces
Group headquarters at Nha Trang, as an intelligence

00:23:33.929 --> 00:23:34.929
officer.

00:23:34.929 --> 00:23:39.649
It was mainly a quiet job, but surprisingly,
he liked it and was soon recognized as the

00:23:39.649 --> 00:23:42.190
leader of Nha Trang's intelligence operations.

00:23:42.190 --> 00:23:45.840
In late June, the base came under attack from
the Vietcong, but the guerrillas did not achieve

00:23:45.840 --> 00:23:47.230
the intended surprise and were swiftly repelled.

00:23:47.230 --> 00:23:50.320
This was thanks to Thorne’s intelligence
work, which had enabled

00:23:50.320 --> 00:23:55.049
“the prompt response of defence elements”,
as stated by his Commanding officer in an

00:23:55.049 --> 00:23:56.049
after action report.

00:23:56.049 --> 00:24:00.710
It was also thanks to this success that Larry
was assigned to lead Operation Shining Brass

00:24:00.710 --> 00:24:01.969
in September.

00:24:01.969 --> 00:24:07.210
This was a secret infiltration of reconnaissance
elements into Laos, a formally neutral country.

00:24:07.210 --> 00:24:12.539
On October 18, 1965, Larry Thorne boarded
a helicopter with South Vietnamese operatives.

00:24:12.539 --> 00:24:16.840
A photo taken that day depicts him as he gets
to the chopper.

00:24:16.840 --> 00:24:22.960
This was the last photo ever taken of Captain
Lauri Torni, reborn in the west as Captain

00:24:22.960 --> 00:24:23.960
Larry Thorne.

00:24:23.960 --> 00:24:28.379
The objective of the mission was to pinpoint
the location of a Communist troop encampment.

00:24:28.379 --> 00:24:32.989
From that perspective, Larry had scored another
success: his team did locate the enemy base,

00:24:32.989 --> 00:24:36.820
and the US dropped 95 tons of bombs on it
two weeks later.

00:24:36.820 --> 00:24:41.879
However, during the flight itself, mission
control lost all contact with Thorne’s helicopter,

00:24:41.879 --> 00:24:44.969
and he and his comrades were declared Missing
in Action.

00:24:44.969 --> 00:24:50.599
Exactly one year later, on October 19, 1966,
they were all declared Killed in Action – although

00:24:50.599 --> 00:24:55.219
neither their bodies, nor the helicopter,
were ever located during the remaining years

00:24:55.219 --> 00:24:56.240
of the Vietnam conflict.

00:24:56.240 --> 00:24:59.360
.
Had he actually died in a helicopter crashed?

00:24:59.360 --> 00:25:03.399
Had he been captured by the Vietcong, or by
the North Vietnamese Army?

00:25:03.399 --> 00:25:07.259
Had he resurfaced again, in another country,
with another name, ready to fight another

00:25:07.259 --> 00:25:08.259
war?

00:25:08.259 --> 00:25:10.739
It took more than 30 years to solve the mystery.

00:25:10.739 --> 00:25:15.789
In 1999, Thorne’s remains were found 25
miles south of Da Nang by a joint Finnish-US

00:25:15.789 --> 00:25:17.049
Task Force.

00:25:17.049 --> 00:25:18.239
He was repatriated to America.

00:25:18.239 --> 00:25:19.239
[Note: Location of Da Nang at this link]

00:25:19.239 --> 00:25:25.409
Formally identified in 2003, his remains were
buried on June 26th, 2003, at Arlington National

00:25:25.409 --> 00:25:29.950
Cemetery, section 60, tombstone 8136.

00:25:29.950 --> 00:25:34.430
He was buried alongside the remaining South
Vietnam Air Force casualties from his final

00:25:34.430 --> 00:25:36.059
mission.

00:25:36.059 --> 00:25:40.970
Remembering Lauri Torni

00:25:40.970 --> 00:25:46.190
In his 47 years of life, Lauri Torni had fought
in three different wars, on behalf of three

00:25:46.190 --> 00:25:51.070
completely different countries, all of which
bestowed upon him praise for his valour.

00:25:51.070 --> 00:25:56.200
He could proudly wear 17 military decorations,
including the Mannherheim Cross, the Iron

00:25:56.200 --> 00:25:59.309
Cross, a Distinguished Flying Cross, and a
Bronze Star.

00:25:59.309 --> 00:26:02.249
To this day, US Special Forces have an award
named after him.

00:26:02.249 --> 00:26:07.179
A former comrade from the Vietnam War, Robin
Moore, took inspiration from Torni for the

00:26:07.179 --> 00:26:10.659
character of Sven Kornie in his book ‘The
Green Berets’.

00:26:10.659 --> 00:26:14.999
This was later turned into a movie, where
the Kornie character would be interpreted

00:26:14.999 --> 00:26:17.609
by ‘The Duke’ himself, John Wayne.

00:26:17.609 --> 00:26:23.369
And in 2014, Lauri was celebrated by metal
band Sabaton, in their song ‘Soldier of

00:26:23.369 --> 00:26:24.369
Three Armies.’

00:26:24.369 --> 00:26:28.330
If you want to know more about the crazy life
of this character, I can recommend the books

00:26:28.330 --> 00:26:33.330
‘Born a Soldier’ by Mike Cleverley and
‘Soldier Under Three Flags’ by Henry A.

00:26:33.330 --> 00:26:34.330
Gill.

00:26:34.330 --> 00:26:38.540
Of course, not everybody agrees on his hero
status: Finnish authors Juha Pohjonen and

00:26:38.540 --> 00:26:43.179
Oula Silvennoinen in their book ‘Unknown
Lauri Torni’ suggest that, at the end of

00:26:43.179 --> 00:26:47.549
WWII, the Finn had joined a Nazi resistance
movement with the purpose of carrying out

00:26:47.549 --> 00:26:50.460
a National Socialist coup in Finland.

00:26:50.460 --> 00:26:54.080
The book is available only in Finnish, but
we’ll leave a link to the eBook in the description

00:26:54.080 --> 00:26:55.779
below, in case you speak the language.

00:26:55.779 --> 00:26:59.399
Controversy aside, I hope you enjoyed today’s
adventurous tale of the Phoenix Soldier.

00:26:59.399 --> 00:27:05.299
As a follow up, let me ask you, which protagonist
of the Winter War would you like us to cover?

